</div></section></section><section aria-label="Article content"><section class="body main-article-body"><section class="abstract" id="ab0010"><h2>Abstract</h2>
<p>High-fidelity volumetric imaging of vascular structures at depth remains challenging for practical biomedical imaging systems, where performance is fundamentally constrained by limited detection aperture and insufficient spatial sampling. Photoacoustic imaging provides label-free vascular contrast at clinically relevant depths, yet achieving high-quality three-dimensional (3D) imaging typically requires complex and customized transducer geometries, limiting accessibility and translational potential. Here, we introduce differential aperture modulation photoacoustic computed tomography (DAM-PACT), a volumetric imaging framework that enhances 3D photoacoustic imaging fidelity using standard linear-array transducers. By synthesizing complementary receive apertures through differential modulation, DAM-PACT increases effective apertures and improves recovery of elevational spatial information, enabling large field-of-view imaging with improved resolution uniformity. Simulations and phantom experiments demonstrate up to four-fold improvement in the elevational resolution without hardware modifications, approaching near-isotropic resolution. In vivo imaging of human arm and palm further shows sharper vascular delineation and improved vessel visibility compared to conventional delay-and-sum reconstruction. By leveraging intrinsic acoustic information in linear-array acquisitions, DAM-PACT provides a practical and scalable strategy for high-fidelity volumetric photoacoustic imaging, with potential for broad adoption in preclinical and clinical settings.</p>
<section id="keys0005" class="kwd-group"><p><strong>Keywords:</strong> Photoacoustic tomography, Volumetric imaging, Vascular imaging, Differential aperture modulation</p></section></section><section id="sec0005"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">1. Introduction</h2>
<p id="p0010">Deep-tissue volumetric imaging plays a central role in biomedical research and clinical diagnostics by enabling visualization of vascular architecture, tissue function, and disease progression across spatial scales [<a href="#bib1" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib1">1</a>,<a href="#bib2" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib2">2</a>]. Such capabilities are essential for applications ranging from tumor angiography [<a href="#bib3" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib3">3</a>] and peripheral vascular assessment [<a href="#bib4" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib4">4</a>,<a href="#bib5" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib5">5</a>] to functional hemodynamic monitoring [<a href="#bib6" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib6">6</a>,<a href="#bib7" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib7">7</a>] and longitudinal disease evaluation [<a href="#bib8" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib8">8</a>,<a href="#bib9" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib9">9</a>]. Optical [<a href="#bib10" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib10">10</a>], acoustic [<a href="#bib11" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib11">11</a>], and hybrid imaging [<a href="#bib12" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib12">12</a>,<a href="#bib13" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib13">13</a>] modalities have each advanced this capability; however, achieving high-resolution three-dimensional (3D) imaging at depth using practical and widely accessible hardware remains a fundamental challenge. In particular, imaging performance in many modalities is ultimately constrained by the spatial sampling of the detection aperture [<a href="#bib14" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib14">14</a>], which governs resolution, field of view, signal sensitivity, and reconstruction fidelity [<a href="#bib15" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib15">[15]</a>, <a href="#bib16" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib16">[16]</a>, <a href="#bib17" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib17">[17]</a>], and therefore defines the attainable image quality in volumetric imaging systems.</p>
<p id="p0015">Photoacoustic computed tomography (PACT) is a promising hybrid modality that combines optical absorption contrast with ultrasonic detection, enabling label-free imaging of deep vascular networks with high optical specificity and acoustic penetration [<a href="#bib18" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib18">18</a>]. While PACT has demonstrated substantial progress in both preclinical and clinical settings [<a href="#bib19" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib19">19</a>,<a href="#bib20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib20">20</a>], including functional and molecular imaging [<a href="#bib21" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib21">[21]</a>, <a href="#bib22" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib22">[22]</a>, <a href="#bib23" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib23">[23]</a>], its extension from two-dimensional (2D) to high-quality 3D imaging remains limited by incomplete angular coverage and insufficient sampling [<a href="#bib20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib20">20</a>,<a href="#bib24" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib24">24</a>]. These limitations are not unique to PACT but reflect a broader challenge in limited-aperture volumetric imaging systems, where restricted detection geometry leads to anisotropic spatial resolution [<a href="#bib25" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib25">25</a>], reduced sensitivity to out-of-plane structures, and degraded reconstruction accuracy [<a href="#bib12" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib12">12</a>]. As a result, achieving uniform and high-fidelity 3D PACT remains difficult without substantially increasing system complexity [<a href="#bib26" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib26">26</a>].</p>
<p id="p0020">To address these limitations, various hardware and computational strategies have been explored. Dense or curved 2D transducer arrays can improve angular coverage and spatial sampling [<a href="#bib27" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib27">[27]</a>, <a href="#bib28" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib28">[28]</a>, <a href="#bib29" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib29">[29]</a>, <a href="#bib30" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib30">[30]</a>, <a href="#bib31" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib31">[31]</a>, <a href="#bib32" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib32">[32]</a>] but typically introduce substantial system complexity, cost, and integration challenges that limit scalability and clinical adoption. Scanning-based approaches and mechanically augmented systems can also enhance sampling density and angular coverage [<a href="#bib33" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib33">[33]</a>, <a href="#bib34" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib34">[34]</a>, <a href="#bib35" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib35">[35]</a>, <a href="#bib36" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib36">[36]</a>, <a href="#bib37" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib37">[37]</a>, <a href="#bib38" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib38">[38]</a>, <a href="#bib39" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib39">[39]</a>] but often do so at the expense of acquisition speed, system robustness, and ease of operation. Data-driven reconstruction methods have shown promise in compensating for incomplete data [<a href="#bib40" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib40">[40]</a>, <a href="#bib41" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib41">[41]</a>, <a href="#bib42" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib42">[42]</a>]; however, they may suffer from limited generalization, dependence on training datasets, and reduced reliability under varying imaging conditions. Collectively, these approaches highlight a critical need for methods that improve volumetric PACT imaging performance without sacrificing system simplicity, robustness, and compatibility with widely available and standardized imaging platforms.</p>
<p id="p0025">Linear-array transducers, which are widely used in clinical ultrasound systems, represent one of the most practical and accessible detection configurations for volumetric ultrasound and PACT imaging [<a href="#bib43" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib43">43</a>,<a href="#bib44" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib44">44</a>]. Their compatibility with existing hardware platforms and established clinical workflows makes them particularly attractive for translational applications [<a href="#bib20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib20">20</a>]. However, their use in 3D PACT imaging is fundamentally constrained by limited elevation aperture and the lack of effective sampling along this dimension, resulting in strongly anisotropic spatial resolution and reduced ability to resolve complex 3D structures [<a href="#bib37" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib37">37</a>,<a href="#bib45" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib45">45</a>,<a href="#bib46" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib46">46</a>]. From a signal acquisition perspective, this limitation arises from insufficient recoverable spatial information and leads to ill-conditioned inverse problems in 3D PACT reconstruction [<a href="#bib47" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib47">47</a>]. Overcoming this limitation without introducing additional hardware complexity remains a key challenge.</p>
<p id="p0030">Aperture-domain strategies provide a promising route to address this challenge by modulating the effective receive aperture to encode additional spatial information into the measured signals [<a href="#bib48" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib48">48</a>]. In ultrasound imaging, such approaches have been explored to improve lateral resolution, contrast, and sensitivity through apodization and synthetic aperture techniques [<a href="#bib49" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib49">[49]</a>, <a href="#bib50" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib50">[50]</a>, <a href="#bib51" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib51">[51]</a>]. However, extending these concepts to volumetric ultrasound imaging remains nontrivial due to the coupled transmission–reception process, where both transmit and receive apertures jointly determine the measured signals [<a href="#bib52" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib52">52</a>]. In contrast, PACT operates under optical excitation with no acoustic transmission, creating a favorable condition in which the initial pressure distribution remains unchanged while the detected signals are governed primarily by the receive aperture. This decoupling offers a unique opportunity to systematically engineer the measurement process through receive-side modulation, enabling controlled encoding of additional spatial information and improved recovery of 3D structures.</p>
<p id="p0035">Here, we introduce differential aperture modulation PACT (DAM-PACT), a volumetric imaging framework that addresses a longstanding limitation of linear-array-based 3D PACT. By applying complementary apodization profiles to the receive aperture and performing differential processing in the envelope domain, DAM-PACT improves recovery of elevational spatial information, thereby narrowing the elevational point-spread function and enhancing resolution uniformity in 3D PACT reconstruction. As a purely post-processing beamforming strategy, it improves image quality without hardware redesign, complex scanning procedures, or model pretraining. Because data acquisition follows the standard workflow of conventional linear-array imaging, the method remains readily compatible with existing platforms and is well suited for practical large-field-of-view, high-fidelity volumetric imaging. Through numerical simulations, phantom experiments, and in vivo human imaging, we demonstrate that DAM-PACT delivers consistent resolution gains and enables high-quality 3D photoacoustic imaging with standard linear-array systems.</p></section><section id="sec0010"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">2. Materials and methods</h2>
<section id="sec0015"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">2.1. System configurations</h3>
<p id="p0040">The DAM-PACT system consisted of a custom linear-array photoacoustic probe, a pulsed laser, a translating scanning stage, a water tank with an imaging window at the bottom, and a data-acquisition system. The photoacoustic probe used a custom 256-channel linear ultrasound transducer for acoustic detection. The transducer specifications were 7.2 MHz center frequency, 0.2-mm pitch, 47% −6 dB fractional bandwidth, 0.18 mm × 4 mm element size, and 20 mm elevational acoustic focal depth. Two linear fiber bundles (A08583, Schott) were mounted on both sides of the transducer and integrated with the array into a 3D-printed probe holder (<a href="#fig0005" class="usa-link">Fig. 1</a>a). The fiber-bundle outputs were aligned to converge at the imaging plane, and the fiber-bundle output width of 51 mm (<a href="#fig0005" class="usa-link">Fig. 1</a>b) was chosen to cover the full lateral field of view of the transducer.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="fig0005"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 1.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/4944533df8b4/gr1.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0005" height="1557" width="1600" alt="Fig. 1"></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/fig0005/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Schematic and workflow of DAM-PACT system. a, Schematic of DAM-PACT using a custom photoacoustic probe; the inset depicts human imaging and the scan direction. b, Photograph of the integrated probe holder consisting of a bifurcated fiber bundle for optical delivery and a linear ultrasound array for acoustic detection. c, Synthetic aperture formation from N adjacent scan positions.<span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M1" altimg="si0001.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> denotes the frame-wise weights from the corresponding apodization function that is applied to <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M2" altimg="si0002.svg"><mi>i</mi></math></span>th RF frame before Delay-and-sum (DAS) beamforming. d, Apodization functions<span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M3" altimg="si0003.svg"><mrow><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">anti</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span>, <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M4" altimg="si0004.svg"><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">biased</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span>, and <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M5" altimg="si0005.svg"><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">biased</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span> applied to <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M6" altimg="si0006.svg"><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">N</mi><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace></mrow></math></span>8 frames within the synthetic aperture, where the 4th frame corresponds to the current elevational location. e, Reconstruction pipeline of DAM-PACT and conventional DAS. <em>S</em>, number of axial samples.</p></figcaption></figure><p id="p0045">Optical excitation was provided by a Q-switched Nd:YAG laser (NT300, EKSPLA) operating at 1064 nm, with a pulse width of 5 ns and a repetition rate of 10 Hz. This configuration delivered a pulse energy of approximately 15 mJ to the imaging plane over a total illumination area of approximately 5 cm², corresponding to a fluence of 3 mJ/cm². This fluence was well below the maximum permissible exposure of 100 mJ/cm² calculated according to the American National Standards Institute (ANSI) standard [<a href="#bib53" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib53">53</a>] for 1064-nm laser exposure.</p>
<p id="p0050">The dual-fiber illumination produced approximately uniform optical excitation within the effective synthetic-aperture region. This uniformity is important because DAM-PACT assumes that neighboring scan positions share a consistent initial-pressure distribution, such that the differences among reconstructed volumes are dominated by receive-aperture modulation rather than illumination variation.</p>
<p id="p0055">For volumetric imaging, the probe was mechanically translated along the elevational direction with a step size of 0.2 mm. The effect of elevational step size on DAM-PACT reconstruction was further evaluated in <a href="#sec0110" class="usa-link">Supplementary Fig. S1</a>. To ensure stable and repeatable acoustic coupling, the probe was immersed in a water tank whose bottom was sealed with an acoustically and optically transparent polyethylene membrane. During in vivo imaging, centrifuged ultrasound gel was applied between the membrane and the tissue surface to minimize bubbles and improve acoustic coupling. Photoacoustic data were acquired using Verasonics Vantage NXT. The Vantage NXT system initiated data acquisition after receiving the laser synchronization output. Potential acquisition jitter was mitigated by aligning the photoacoustic signal generated by scattered light absorbed at the surface of the probe. All system control and data acquisition were implemented using a custom MATLAB interface (R2022b, MathWorks, USA). Raw radiofrequency (RF) data were acquired at a sampling rate of 33.3 MHz without hardware time-gain compensation.</p></section><section id="sec0020"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">2.2. DAM-PACT reconstruction principle</h3>
<p id="p0060">DAM-PACT reconstruction was performed on RF data acquired from sequential elevational scan positions. For each target elevational location, DAM-PACT forms a local synthetic aperture by aggregating RF data from neighboring scan positions. Along the elevational direction, the synthetic aperture comprised up to <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M7" altimg="si0010.svg"><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">N</mi><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mn>8</mn></mrow></math></span> neighboring frames centered on the target position (<a href="#fig0005" class="usa-link">Fig. 1</a>c). At scan boundaries, when fewer than four frames were available on one side, the window size was reduced to maintain symmetry about the center. With an elevational receive angle of approximately 4.3°, <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M8" altimg="si0011.svg"><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">N</mi><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><mn>8</mn></mrow></math></span> was selected for an imaging depth of 20 mm.</p>
<p id="p0065">Within each synthetic-aperture window, three complementary elevational apodization profiles were applied to reconstruct three beamformed volumes using DAS, with the corresponding apodization coefficient <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M9" altimg="si0012.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> applied to each <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M10" altimg="si0013.svg"><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span> frame during beamforming (<a href="#fig0005" class="usa-link">Fig. 1</a>c). The weighted DAS reconstruction for an apodization <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M11" altimg="si0014.svg"><mi>W</mi></math></span> profile can be expressed as</p>
<table class="disp-formula p" id="eqn0005"><tr>
<td class="formula"><math id="M12" display="block" altimg="si0015.svg"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mfenced open="(" close=")"><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold-italic">r</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mrow><mfenced open="|" close="|"><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">H</mi><mrow><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">elem</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mfenced open="[" close="]"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold-italic">r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow></math></td>
<td class="label">(1)</td>
</tr></table>
<p>Where <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M13" altimg="si0016.svg"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span> denotes the RF signal received by the <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M14" altimg="si0017.svg"><mi>m</mi></math></span>th transducer element in the <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M15" altimg="si0018.svg"><mi>i</mi></math></span>th frame, and <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M16" altimg="si0019.svg"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold-italic">r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span> denotes the acoustic time delay corresponding to reconstruction location <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M17" altimg="si0020.svg"><mi mathvariant="bold-italic">r</mi></math></span>, the <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M18" altimg="si0021.svg"><mi>i</mi></math></span>th scan position and the <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M19" altimg="si0022.svg"><mi>m</mi></math></span>th transducer element. <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M20" altimg="si0023.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">elem</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> denotes the number of transducer elements, and <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M21" altimg="si0024.svg"><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">H</mi><mrow><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mo>⋅</mo></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow></math></span> denotes the Hilbert-transform-based envelope operation, <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M22" altimg="si0025.svg"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace></mrow></math></span>denotes the frame-wise apodization coefficient assigned to the <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M23" altimg="si0026.svg"><mi>i</mi></math></span>th RF frame under apodization profile<span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M24" altimg="si0027.svg"><mrow><mspace width="0.75em"></mspace><mi>W</mi></mrow></math></span>. These profiles included one antisymmetric weighting, <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M25" altimg="si0028.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">anti</mi></mrow></msub></math></span>, and two direct-current (DC)-shifted biased weightings, <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M26" altimg="si0029.svg"><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">biased</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span> and<span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M27" altimg="si0030.svg"><mrow><mspace width="0.25em"></mspace><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">biased</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></span> (<a href="#fig0005" class="usa-link">Fig. 1</a>d). The antisymmetric weighting was defined as</p>
<table class="disp-formula p" id="eqn0010"><tr>
<td class="formula"><math id="M28" display="block" altimg="si0031.svg"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">anti</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mfenced open="{"><mrow><mtable><mtr><mtd><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow></math></td>
<td class="label">(2)</td>
</tr></table>
<p>where <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M29" altimg="si0032.svg"><mi>i</mi></math></span> denotes the frame index within the synthetic aperture and N is an even integer representing the number of neighboring frames used to construct the synthetic aperture. Alternative tapering window strategies were also evaluated, as shown in <a href="#sec0110" class="usa-link">Supplementary Fig. S2</a>. The two biased weightings constructed as</p>
<table class="disp-formula p" id="eqn0015"><tr>
<td class="formula"><math id="M30" display="block" altimg="si0033.svg"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">biased</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">anti</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo linebreak="badbreak">+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math></td>
<td class="label">(3)</td>
</tr></table>
<table class="disp-formula p" id="eqn0020"><tr>
<td class="formula"><math id="M31" display="block" altimg="si0034.svg"><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">biased</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mfenced open="(" close=")"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mo linebreak="badbreak">−</mo><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">anti</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo linebreak="badbreak">+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math></td>
<td class="label">(4)</td>
</tr></table>
<p>where δ is a tunable DC offset. The RF frames within each synthetic aperture window were first phase-aligned to preserve inter-frame phase consistency. DAS beamforming was then performed using <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M32" altimg="si0035.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">anti</mi></mrow></msub></math></span>, <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M33" altimg="si0036.svg"><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">biased</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span>, and <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M34" altimg="si0037.svg"><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">biased</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span> followed by a Hilbert-transform-based envelope detection to obtain envelope images <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M35" altimg="si0038.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">anti</mi></mrow></msub></math></span>, <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M36" altimg="si0039.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub></math></span>, and <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M37" altimg="si0040.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub></math></span>. The DAM-PACT enhanced envelope was computed by a noncoherent differential combination:</p>
<table class="disp-formula p" id="eqn0025"><tr>
<td class="formula"><math id="M38" display="block" altimg="si0041.svg"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">DAM</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo linebreak="badbreak">+</mo><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></msub><mo linebreak="badbreak">−</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">anti</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td>
<td class="label">(5)</td>
</tr></table>
<p id="p0070">The two biased weightings introduce a small positive bridge across this null region, allowing both volumes to recover the true structure along with the sidelobes <strong>(</strong><a href="#sec0110" class="usa-link">Supplementary Fig. S3</a><strong>)</strong>. Mathematically, the complex elevational aperture responses before envelope detection for the uniform and antisymmetric aperture, denoted by <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M39" altimg="si0042.svg"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mfenced open="(" close=")"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow></math></span> and <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M40" altimg="si0043.svg"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mfenced open="(" close=")"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow></math></span>, are</p>
<table class="disp-formula p" id="eqn0030"><tr>
<td class="formula"><math id="M41" display="block" altimg="si0044.svg"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mfenced open="(" close=")"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">iq</mi><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><msub><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mfenced open="(" close=")"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">anti</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">iq</mi><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></math></td>
<td class="label">(6)</td>
</tr></table>
<p>Where <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M42" altimg="si0045.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> is the elevational position of the <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M43" altimg="si0046.svg"><mi>i</mi></math></span>th frame and <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M44" altimg="si0047.svg"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">q</mi><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">π</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi>sin</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">θ</mi></mrow></math></span> represents the phase mismatch from the central frame. The corresponding beamformed signals are <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M45" altimg="si0048.svg"><mrow><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span> and <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M46" altimg="si0049.svg"><mrow><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></span>. Thus, <a href="#eqn0020" class="usa-link">Equation (4)</a> becomes</p>
<table class="disp-formula p" id="eqn0035"><tr>
<td class="formula"><math id="M47" display="block" altimg="si0050.svg"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">DAM</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mfenced open="(" close=")"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mrow><mfenced open="|" close="|"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mfenced open="[" close="]"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mfenced open="|" close="|"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mfenced open="(" close=")"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mfenced open="|" close="|"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mfenced open="(" close=")"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo linebreak="badbreak">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">|</mi><msub><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">|</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mrow></math></td>
<td class="label">(7)</td>
</tr></table>
<p id="p0075">A more detailed derivation is provided in <a href="#sec0110" class="usa-link">Supplementary Note 1</a>, where we present a mathematical analysis showing that the true structure is preserved while the sidelobes are suppressed after differential combination. The differential combination therefore cancels the sidelobe contributions common to all three volumes while preserving the true structure that is absent only in the antisymmetric volume, effectively narrowing the elevational point-spread function. The reconstruction pipeline is illustrated in <a href="#fig0005" class="usa-link">Fig. 1</a>e. Conventional DAS beamforming served as the baseline method, in which each elevational frame was reconstructed independently, and the resulting frames were stacked to form the final volume. For our linear array, we also compared the DAS stack method and 2D DAS reconstruction via synthetic aperture, which showed slightly worse images (<a href="#sec0110" class="usa-link">Supplementary Fig. S4</a>). All reconstruction computations were performed in MATLAB (R2025a, MathWorks, USA) using an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4060 graphics processing unit for accelerated image reconstruction.</p></section><section id="sec0025"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">2.3. Numerical simulation</h3>
<p id="p0080">Numerical validation was performed using the k-Wave toolbox in MATLAB (R2025a, MathWorks, USA). To accurately replicate the experimental conditions, the virtual linear array was modeled using geometric and acoustic parameters derived from the actual probe used in the experiments. The simulation phantom was positioned parallel to the transducer surface at depths of 10 mm, 15 mm, 20 mm, and 25 mm, respectively. For the beam pattern simulation, we modeled an array comprising 83 elements along the x direction and 8 rows along the y direction. Element directivity in the y direction was explicitly incorporated with a −6 dB acceptance half-angle of 2.15°.</p></section><section id="sec0030"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">2.4. Phantom preparation</h3>
<p id="p0085">Phantoms were fabricated using an agarose-based matrix to emulate the acoustic properties of soft tissue. Agarose (1.5% w/v) was dissolved in deionized water, heated until optically transparent, and cast into molds after the target was embedded. For hair phantoms, targets were first positioned and secured with a 3D-printed mold featuring small holes. Locations of these small holes were defined by the desired insertion angle and depth of the hair. The agarose solution was poured to embed targets in a single casting step. For other non-hair phantoms, the structures were directly fabricated by 3D printing using black polylactic acid. These printed structures were embedded and sealed using a two-step agarose procedure. First, a base layer was poured and allowed to partially gel. The structure was then inserted, followed by a second agarose layer to encapsulate and seal the phantom.</p></section><section id="sec0035"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">2.5. Inter-frame phase correction</h3>
<p id="p0090">During in vivo scanning, motion-induced phase mismatch is often observed. The correction process was initiated from the central frame of the synthetic aperture and propagated bidirectionally toward the edges. Axial alignment was determined by maximizing Pearson correlation coefficients between the current frame and a cumulative reference derived from previously aligned frames. To ensure alignment accuracy, the calculation of Pearson correlation coefficients was restricted to a specific region of interest in B-scan images that contained most of the photoacoustic signals from tissue. A constrained search window of ±5 samples was used to identify the optimal axial shift, avoiding over-correction due to outliers. After calculation, the optimal axial shift was applied as additional time delays in the signal domain via truncation and zero-padding to preserve the original data length. The aligned dataset was incorporated into the cumulative reference for the subsequent frame, ensuring robust coherence within the synthetic aperture.</p></section><section id="sec0040"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">2.6. Skin and reverberation removal</h3>
<p id="p0095">Strong optical absorption at the skin often generates dominant photoacoustic signals that obscure deep vascular structures during visualization. Acoustic reverberations between the probe surface and the skin interface could also create strong reflection artifacts appearing at twice the transducer-skin distance. To mitigate these issues, a semi-automated segmentation algorithm was developed to extract and remove the skin layer and its associated reverberations. The skin boundary was first roughly identified by manual selection in several selected frames, then refined by searching for the maximum intensity within a narrow ±10-pixel window. Based on these refined priors, the skin boundary in intermediate frames was first roughly identified by interpolation, then refined using the same maximum-intensity search procedure. Reverberation artifact axial positions, denoted by <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M48" altimg="si0051.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">echo</mi></mrow></msub></math></span>, were estimated based on the axial positions of the probe <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M49" altimg="si0052.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">probe</mi></mrow></msub></math></span> and the skin surface positions <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M50" altimg="si0053.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">skin</mi></mrow></msub></math></span>. Assuming a round-trip acoustic path, the reverberation artifact depth was calculated as <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M51" altimg="si0054.svg"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">echo</mi></mrow></msub><mo linebreak="goodbreak">=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">skin</mi></mrow></msub><mo linebreak="badbreak">−</mo><msub><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">probe</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math></span>. Signals corresponding to both the extracted skin layer and the calculated reverberation zone were suppressed to reveal underlying vasculature.</p></section></section><section id="sec0045"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">3. Results</h2>
<section id="sec0050"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">3.1. Simulation experiments results</h3>
<p id="p0100">We first validated DAM-PACT using numerical simulations implemented in the k-Wave toolbox [<a href="#bib54" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib54">54</a>], with the virtual system configured to closely match our experimental probe and imaging geometry. To interrogate elevational performance over depth, multi-depth line-target simulations were performed with targets placed parallel to the transducer face at 10, 15, 20, and 25 mm (<a href="#fig0010" class="usa-link">Fig. 2</a>a). For beam-pattern characterization, we modeled a sub-aperture of 83 elements along the lateral (<em>x</em>) direction and 8 rows along the elevational (<em>y</em>) direction, corresponding to effective apertures of 16.6 mm (lateral) and 1.4 mm (elevational). The beam patterns were subsequently computed from this sub-aperture configuration (<a href="#fig0010" class="usa-link">Fig. 2</a>b, c).</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="fig0010"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 2.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/32d68cee7351/gr2.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0010" height="904" width="1600" alt="Fig. 2"></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/fig0010/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Simulation validation of DAM-PACT. a, Multi-depth line-target simulation visualized by y–depth projections, where DAM-PACT yields a visibly narrower elevational response across depths (<span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M52" altimg="si0007.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> = 0.1). b, Beam patterns generated using three weighting schemes. c, Angular beam patterns for DAS and the DAM-PACT. d, Depth-dependent elevational resolution quantified from (a), comparing DAS and DAM-PACT. e, Central lateral line profiles corresponding to the comparison in (c), showing that DAM-PACT does not affect the lateral response. f, Central elevational line profiles corresponding to the comparison in (c), showing that the measured FWHM beamwidth decreases from 1.37 mm to 0.33 mm.</p></figcaption></figure><p id="p0105">Across the depth range in <a href="#fig0010" class="usa-link">Fig. 2</a>a, depth-dependent analysis demonstrated that DAM-PACT consistently improved elevational resolution throughout the imaging region relative to conventional DAS (<a href="#fig0010" class="usa-link">Fig. 2</a>d), with the effective performance being dependent on the input SNR (<a href="#sec0110" class="usa-link">Supplementary Fig. S5</a>). Central line profiles showed an unchanged lateral beamwidth (<a href="#fig0010" class="usa-link">Fig. 2</a>e), but a narrower elevational main lobe with DAM-PACT (<a href="#fig0010" class="usa-link">Fig. 2</a>f). The corresponding full width at half maximum (FWHM) values were 0.30 mm (lateral) and 0.33 mm (elevational), indicating near-isotropic lateral and elevational resolution with DAM-PACT. A small <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M53" altimg="si0055.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> of 0.1 was used in simulation to demonstrate the maximum achievable resolution gain under ideal, noise-free conditions. In practice, experimental factors such as finite SNR and motion-induced phase errors necessitate a larger <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M54" altimg="si0056.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> to maintain structural fidelity, as characterized in the following phantom study.</p></section><section id="sec0055"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">3.2. Phantom-based optimization and validation of DAM-PACT</h3>
<p id="p0110">We experimentally validated DAM-PACT in a set of phantom studies to establish a practical operating parameter and assess its performance under realistic system conditions. In DAM-PACT, the differential results are controlled by the parameter <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M55" altimg="si0057.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span>, which introduces a trade-off between elevational resolution gain and structural fidelity. <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M56" altimg="si0058.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> is a small DC offset used to construct <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M57" altimg="si0059.svg"><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">biased</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span>, and <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M58" altimg="si0060.svg"><msubsup><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">biased</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span> from <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M59" altimg="si0061.svg"><msub><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="italic">anti</mi></mrow></msub></math></span>. Smaller <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M60" altimg="si0062.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> increases the differential effect and tightens elevational confinement but may also attenuate true structures. Conversely, larger <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M61" altimg="si0063.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> yields a more conservative reconstruction that gradually approaches conventional DAS, with correspondingly weaker elevational improvement (<a href="#fig0015" class="usa-link">Fig. 3</a>a).</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="fig0015"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 3.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/df2fbe66bd36/gr3.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0015" height="1119" width="1600" alt="Fig. 3"></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/fig0015/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Phantom validation of DAM-PACT. a, DAM-PACT reconstructions of a cross-hair phantom with different <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M62" altimg="si0008.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> values, along with the corresponding DAS. b, Dependence of image metrics on <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M63" altimg="si0009.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span>: lateral and elevational resolution (left), and SSIM together with the BAR metric (right). c, <em>x–y</em> projections of a multi-depth hair phantom, where DAM-PACT preserves in-plane continuity while tightening the elevational response. d, Star-pattern resolution phantom, where DAM-PACT circularizes the spectral support, consistent with a more balanced angular response. e, Elevational resolution quantified at different depths in (c). f, Line profiles extracted along the marked line in (d), demonstrating improved resolvability of adjacent structures with DAM-PACT.</p></figcaption></figure><p id="p0115">We first swept <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M64" altimg="si0064.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> using a cross-hair phantom to visualize how differential strength impacts resolution and structural preservation (<a href="#fig0015" class="usa-link">Fig. 3</a>a). At low <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M65" altimg="si0065.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span>, elevational sidelobes and off-axis leakage were strongly suppressed; as <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M66" altimg="si0066.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> increased, the differential effect weakened and the point response gradually resembled DAS. We then quantified these trends using resolution and structural metrics (<a href="#fig0015" class="usa-link">Fig. 3</a>b). As <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M67" altimg="si0067.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> decreased, the elevational resolution improved monotonically while the lateral resolution remained largely unchanged, yielding a beam aspect ratio (BAR, defined as the lateral-to-elevational resolution ratio) closer to unity and thus a more balanced spatial response. However, excessively low <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M68" altimg="si0068.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> also attenuated true structures, as reflected by a decrease in the structural similarity index measure (SSIM). We therefore selected <span xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><math id="M69" altimg="si0069.svg"><mi>δ</mi></math></span> = 0.5 as the operating point for subsequent <em>in vivo</em> reconstructions, balancing elevational resolution gain against structural fidelity. The corresponding trade-off was further evaluated using in vivo data (<a href="#sec0110" class="usa-link">Supplementary Fig. S6</a>).</p>
<p id="p0120">We next evaluated DAM-PACT on a multi-depth line-target phantom to investigate its performance across imaging depths (<a href="#fig0015" class="usa-link">Fig. 3</a>c). Compared with DAS, DAM-PACT consistently narrows the elevational response, yielding sharper boundaries and a uniform thickness across depths. Quantitative analysis confirmed a stable reduction in elevational width over the tested depth range and mitigated the depth-dependent degradation observed with DAS (<a href="#fig0015" class="usa-link">Fig. 3</a>e).</p>
<p id="p0125">Finally, we imaged a star-pattern resolution phantom to examine the angular response and resolution isotropy (<a href="#fig0015" class="usa-link">Fig. 3</a>c). In the spatial domain, DAM-PACT clearly separated radial spokes. In the frequency domain, the corresponding 2D Fourier magnitude spectrum exhibited a more circular spectrum with eccentricity reduced from 0.90 to 0.66, indicating a more isotropic resolution. Line profiles extracted along the marked cut corroborated these observations by showing improved separability and contrast between adjacent structures (<a href="#fig0015" class="usa-link">Fig. 3</a>f). Together, these phantom results demonstrate that DAM-PACT achieves robust enhancement of elevational confinement.</p></section><section id="sec0060"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">3.3. Skin-signal removal and motion-induced phase mismatch correction for in vivo 3D DAM-PACT</h3>
<p id="p0130">Strong photoacoustic signals originating from the skin often saturate the 2D maximum-intensity-projection (MIP) images, obscuring underlying vascular architecture in PACT visualization. To mitigate this, we implemented a skin-signal removal step to filter superficial signal contamination, revealing the underlying vessels clearly (<a href="#fig0020" class="usa-link">Fig. 4</a>a). Detailed procedures of dynamic skin removal can be found in the Methods. Additionally, during <em>in vivo</em> imaging via linear-array scanning, subtle subject motion, probe micro-drift, and physiological motion (e.g., respiration) can introduce non-negligible frame-to-frame phase mismatches in the RF data. In synthetic aperture reconstruction, these mismatches are amplified and appear as band-like dropouts (e.g., dark bands), which can locally break vessel continuity and reduce the reliability of the vasculature quantification (<a href="#fig0020" class="usa-link">Fig. 4</a>b).</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="fig0020"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 4.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/8d87e8f3e635/gr4.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0020" height="1105" width="1600" alt="Fig. 4"></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/fig0020/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Skin-signal removal and motion artifact correction in linear-array-based 3D DAM-PACT. a, <em>In vivo</em> DAM-PACT of vasculature before (left) and after (right) skin removal. Superficial skin signals obscure vessel visibility, which is improved after dynamic skin-signal removal. b, <em>In vivo</em> DAM-PACT of vasculature before (left) and after (right) correction. Phase mismatch produces black bands indicated by arrows, which are reduced after correction. c, Pearson correlation matrices of frame-to-frame RF data within one synthetic aperture (i.e., 8 frames). Pronounced phase inversions (i.e., negative correlation) disappear after correction, and the overall correlations increase. d, Distribution of the minimum Pearson correlation coefficient within each synthetic aperture. Phase correction shifts the distribution toward higher correlation values, indicating reduced phase mismatch.</p></figcaption></figure><p id="p0135">To illustrate the effect, we analyzed a representative synthetic aperture centered at frame 51. Before correction, the frame-to-frame correlation matrix showed prominent variability and regions of negative correlation, indicating phase inversion. For each neighboring frame, we searched for a small axial shift that maximizes the Pearson correlation coefficient with a cumulative reference frame within a predefined depth window and applied this shift to correct inter-frame axial misalignment, as described in detail in the Methods. After correction, negative correlations were largely removed and the overall correlation increased, indicating restored phase coherence within the synthetic aperture (<a href="#fig0020" class="usa-link">Fig. 4</a>c). Across all synthetic apertures, motion correction increased the minimum Pearson correlation and nearly eliminated negative correlations (<a href="#fig0020" class="usa-link">Fig. 4</a>d). Consistent with these correlation-based metrics, the corrected PACT images show substantially reduced banding artifacts and a more continuous and structurally complete vascular network (<a href="#fig0020" class="usa-link">Fig. 4</a>b).</p></section><section id="sec0065"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">3.4. <em>In vivo</em> DAM-PACT of human forearm and palm vasculature</h3>
<p id="p0140">We further evaluated DAM-PACT in vivo by imaging human vasculature. 3D photoacoustic datasets from the forearm and palm regions were reconstructed using conventional DAS and DAM-PACT, followed by MIP visualization of the vascular network (<a href="#fig0025" class="usa-link">Fig. 5</a>a,b). Compared with DAS, DAM-PACT produced sharper vessel boundaries and continuous depiction of fine vessels across the field of view, while substantially reducing the patchy elevational leakage that is commonly observed in DAS reconstructions. These differences are highlighted in several selected close-up regions (R1–R5) (<a href="#fig0025" class="usa-link">Fig. 5</a><strong>c</strong>), where DAM-PACT separates adjacent and partially overlapping vessels which are indistinguishable in DAS. In particular, palmar vessels (R2) and forearm vessels (R5) are clearly separated with fewer spurious connections attributable to elevational leakage.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="fig0025"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 5.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/2a9ddb5cb741/gr5.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0025" height="2308" width="1600" alt="Fig. 5"></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/fig0025/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p><em>In vivo</em> DAM-PACT of human forearm and palm vasculature. a, <em>In vivo</em> DAS based PACT of human palm and forearm vessels. b, <em>In vivo</em> DAM-PACT of human palm and forearm vessels. c, Close-up views of selected areas R1–R5 marked by dashed boxes in (a). Arrows indicate some representative PVs and FVs that are better separated by DAM-PACT. PV, palmar vessel; FV, forearm vessel. d, Line profiles extracted across representative vessels (vessels #1–#4) marked by dotted line in (a), showing a clearer separation with DAM-PACT compared to DAS. e, Box-and-whisker plots of palm vessel (palms #1 and #2) resolution measured by the selected vessels marked by short white ticks in (a), demonstrating a consistent <em>in vivo</em> resolution improvement with DAM-PACT. Vessels were selected to sample a range of orientations within each region.</p></figcaption></figure><p id="p0145">To quantify the improvement, we extracted normalized intensity profiles across representative vessel cross-sections (vessels #1–#4 in <a href="#fig0025" class="usa-link">Fig. 5</a>a). Compared with DAS, DAM-PACT had better resolvability (<a href="#fig0025" class="usa-link">Fig. 5</a>d). We further summarized resolution measurements across multiple palm vessel cross-sections, corresponding to palm #1 and #2 in <a href="#fig0025" class="usa-link">Fig. 5</a>a using box plots (<a href="#fig0025" class="usa-link">Fig. 5</a>e), which confirmed consistent resolution improvement, with an improved FWHM from 1.162 mm to 0.541 mm and 1.286 mm to 0.604 mm, respectively. It is worth noting that band-like artifacts are reduced but not fully eliminated after phase correction, likely because a subset of frames exhibits non-rigid motion that cannot be fully compensated by axial shifting alone.</p></section></section><section id="sec0070"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">4. Discussion</h2>
<p id="p0150">Achieving high-fidelity volumetric imaging with practical and widely deployable hardware remains a central challenge across biomedical imaging modalities. In this work, we demonstrate that DAM-PACT, a physics-guided and training-free beamforming framework, can improve elevational resolution and volumetric image fidelity in linear-array-based 3D PACT. This improvement was consistently validated across numerical simulations, phantom experiments, and in vivo human imaging. As shown in the results, DAM-PACT produced sharper vessel boundaries, reduced elevational leakage, and improved separation of overlapping vascular structures compared with conventional delay-and-sum reconstruction. Notably, under a standard linear-scan workflow with no additional acquisition, DAM-PACT achieved up to four-fold improvement in simulation and phantom, which reduced to a two-fold improvement in vivo potentially due to the motion artifact, enabling more uniform and interpretable 3D PACT imaging.</p>
<p id="p0155">Compared with existing 3D PACT approaches, DAM-PACT offers a key practical advantage in its versatility and system compatibility. Rather than relying on specialized hardware architectures or complex scanning schemes, the method operates directly on standard linear-array acquisitions and can be adapted to a wide range of systems through probe-specific modulation parameter adjustment. This flexibility makes DAM-PACT particularly attractive for translational and clinical settings, where hardware simplicity and robustness are critical. Focal-line-based reconstruction is another approach that improves elevational resolution in focused transducer array by incorporating the intrinsic elevational focal geometry into 3D reconstruction [<a href="#bib55" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib55">55</a>]. In comparison, DAM-PACT achieves elevational resolution enhancement through a distinct reconstruction principle, allowing it to be extended to non-focused transducer arrays in PACT. Notably, non-focused arrays generally provide broader elevational angular coverage and can therefore support a larger effective synthetic aperture, which may further increase the potential resolution gain achievable with DAM-PACT. In addition, combining DAM-PACT with high-frequency linear arrays may further enhance spatial resolution for small-animal or superficial imaging. More broadly, extending the differential modulation strategy to both lateral and elevational dimensions, for example using 2D matrix arrays, could enable simultaneous resolution enhancement along both lateral and elevational axes within a unified acquisition framework.</p>
<p id="p0160">Despite the improvements demonstrated, several limitations of DAM-PACT should be considered. First, the DC offset parameter δ must be tuned according to imaging conditions, and a phantom with a comparable SNR could be used to guide parameter selection in cases where ground truth is unavailable, such as in vivo imaging. Under low signal-to-noise ratio conditions, a small δ may reduce resolution enhancement while introducing structural attenuation; in our in vivo experiments with signal-to-noise ratio better than 15 dB, δ = 0.5 provided a practical balance between elevational sharpening and structural fidelity. Second, the synthetic aperture reconstruction relies on sufficient inter-frame phase coherence [<a href="#bib46" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib46">46</a>], making the method sensitive to motion. Significant motion can degrade the differential combination and reconstruction quality. Incorporating motion-aware alignment strategies [<a href="#bib56" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib56">56</a>], such as non-rigid compensation informed by physiological dynamics [<a href="#bib57" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="bib57">57</a>], represents a promising direction for improving robustness. Third, DAM-PACT currently requires offline reconstruction due to the need for synthetic aperture formation and multiple beamforming operations. However, the structured nature of the pipeline makes it well suited for acceleration using GPU-based beamforming, which could enable near real-time or interactive reconstruction in future implementations. Besides, while the present study focuses on technical validation of DAM-PACT, its diagnostic value remains to be established in disease-specific studies. Future work should determine whether the improved vessel delineation and elevational resolution enable more reliable quantification of vascular features in clinically relevant disease models or patient populations.</p>
<p id="p0165">Additionally, the performance of DAM-PACT may be further improved with alternative transducer configurations. For example, the focused elevational lens of the probe used in this study limits the effective elevational acceptance angle and available aperture. An unfocused configuration could provide broader angular coverage and a larger effective aperture, thereby allowing a larger effective N to be used in reconstruction, potentially improve sidelobe suppression and elevational resolution, while allowing greater flexibility for tapering window strategies. Beyond resolution enhancement, DAM-PACT is inherently compatible with conventional ultrasound imaging workflows, enabling seamless integration with ultrasound-based modalities such as ultrafast imaging. This multimodal compatibility may expand the utility of DAM-PACT for combined structural and functional imaging. More generally, the differential aperture modulation strategy provides a practical means of increasing measurement accuracy in linear-array-based systems, offering a pathway toward improved volumetric PACT imaging performance without increasing hardware complexity.</p></section><section id="sec0075"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Code availability</h2>
<p id="p0170">The code used to process data will be provided upon reasonable request.</p></section><section id="sec0080"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Author contributions</h2>
<p id="p0175">R.C., M.H., and Z.K. conceived the DAM-PACT concept. M.H., Z.G. and X.M. designed the customized probe holder. M.H. developed the experimental setup. M.H. and Z.K. developed the DAM-PACT processing code. M.H. and M.M. conducted simulations, phantom experiments, and in vivo imaging, and performed validation and visualization. M.J. and M.O. provided guidance on data interpretation and manuscript preparation. M.H., Z.K., and R.C. wrote the manuscript with input from all authors.</p></section><section id="sec0085"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">CRediT authorship contribution statement</h2>
<p id="p0180"><strong>Muhang He:</strong> Writing – review &amp; editing, Writing – original draft, Visualization, Validation, Software, Methodology, Investigation, Data curation, Conceptualization. <strong>Zhengchang Kou:</strong> Writing – review &amp; editing, Supervision, Software, Methodology, Conceptualization. <strong>Maria F. Michalska:</strong> Writing – review &amp; editing, Visualization, Software, Investigation, Formal analysis. <strong>Zhouyan Gao:</strong> Writing – review &amp; editing, Data curation. <strong>Xinru Meng:</strong> Writing – review &amp; editing, Data curation. <strong>Michael W. Jenkins:</strong> Writing – review &amp; editing, Supervision, Resources. <strong>Michael L. Oelze:</strong> Writing – review &amp; editing, Supervision. <strong>Rui Cao:</strong> Writing – review &amp; editing, Supervision, Resources, Project administration, Methodology, Funding acquisition, Conceptualization.</p></section><section id="sec0090"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Ethics declaration</h2>
<p id="p0185">Written informed consent to take part in the study and to publish the article has been obtained from all participants or their legal representatives. The privacy rights of participants have been observed.</p>
<p id="p0190">This study was performed in compliance with relevant laws, regulatory frameworks and guidelines where the research took place. This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of Case Western Reserve University. (Approval No. STUDY20250833)</p></section><section id="sec0095"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Ethical statement</h2>
<p id="p0195">All human measurements complied with the Declaration of Helsinki and were approved by the Institutional Review Board of Case Western Reserve University (Protocol No. STUDY20250833). Written informed consent was obtained from all subjects before experimentation. The study utilized non-invasive photoacoustic imaging with laser fluence well within ANSI safety limits. To protect participant privacy, no personal identifiers were recorded.</p></section><section id="sec0100"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Funding</h2>
<p id="p0200">R.C. was sponsored by the United States National Institutes of Health grants R00 EB034298 and R01 EB037413, American Heart Association Innovative Project Award
26IPA162099, and Case Western Reserve University Startup Funds. Z.G. was sponsored by Case Western Reserve University Sponsored Summer Research Program.</p></section><section id="coi0005"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Declaration of Competing Interest</h2>
<p id="p0205">The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.</p></section><section id="_bios93_" lang="en"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Biographies</h2>
<section id="bio1" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/1446d6c3932f/fx1.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0035" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx1.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Muhang He</strong> received his B.Eng. degree in Biomedical Engineering from Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China, in 2024. He is currently a graduate student in the department of Biomedical Engineering at Case Western Reserve University, Ohio, USA. His current research interests include photoacoustic tomography, ultrasound image reconstruction, and computational imaging techniques for biomedical applications.</p></section><section id="bio2" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/8843ed3efe3d/fx2.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0040" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx2.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Zhengchang Kou</strong> received the B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. degrees in electrical and computer engineering from the University of Illinois Urbana–Champaign (UIUC), Urbana, IL, USA, in 2017, 2020, and 2023, respectively. He is currently a Postdoctoral Research Associate at the Beckman Institute for Advanced Science and Technology, UIUC, supported by the Beckman Institute Postdoctoral Fellowship. His research focuses on ultrafast ultrasound imaging, contrast-free super-resolution imaging, FPGA-based hardware acceleration, ultrasound imaging systems, and wearable ultrasound technologies</p></section><section id="bio3" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/8dca617fba80/fx3.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0045" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx3.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Maria F. Michalska</strong> is an undergraduate student at Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, Ohio, USA. She is currently pursuing a bachelor’s degree in biomedical engineering. Her research interests include computational methods and photoacoustic microscopy.</p></section><section id="bio4" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/81aa1f69dc1e/fx4.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0050" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx4.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Zhouyan Gao</strong> is a graduate student in the Department of Biomedical Engineering at Duke University. She received the B.S.E. degree from Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH, USA, in 2026. Her current research focuses on medical imaging and biomedical instrumentation, with interests in the development of imaging systems and technologies for healthcare applications.</p></section><section id="bio5" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/b2c3cccdf615/fx5.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0055" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx5.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Xinru Meng</strong> was an undergraduate student at Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, Ohio, USA. She received the B.S. degree in biomedical engineering and electrical engineering from Case Western Reserve University in 2026. Her research is focused on ultrasound imaging.</p></section><section id="bio6" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/b09d16fe1cc9/fx6.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0060" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx6.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Michael W. Jenkins</strong> is the Dr. Donald and Ruth Weber Goodman Professor of Innovative Cardiovascular Research in the Department of Biomedical Engineering at Case Western Reserve University (CWRU), with a part-time appointment at the FES Center within the Louis Stokes Cleveland VA Medical Center. He leads a well funded interdisciplinary biomedical optics laboratory with two main branches. His imaging research develops novel optical systems, spatial transcriptomic analysis, and AI methods for image reconstruction and predictive modeling. Separately, his therapeutics research pioneers treatments using transient selective neural inhibition via photobiomodulation (tSNIP) and photobiomodulation. This work spans cardiovascular development, chronic pain, neurodegenerative diseases, ocular surface pathologies, and oncology. Additionally, he directs the CWRU School of Medicine Light Microscopy Imaging Core.</p></section><section id="bio7" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/72db4a96b115/fx7.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0065" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx7.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Michael L. Oelze</strong> was born in Hamilton, New Zealand, in 1971. He received the B.S. degree in physics and mathematics from Harding University, Searcy, AR, USA, in 1994, and the Ph.D. degree in physics from The University of Mississippi, Oxford, MS, USA, in 2000.From 2000–2002, he was a Postdoctoral Researcher with the Bioacoustics Research Laboratory, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE), University of Illinois at Urbana–Champaign (UIUC), Urbana, IL, USA. From 2002–2004, he was an NIH Fellow at UIUC, conducting research in quantitative ultrasound techniques for biomedical ultrasound applications in cancer detection. In 2005, he joined the Faculty of Electrical and Computer Engineering, UIUC, where he is currently a Professor, a Health Innovator Professor with the Carle Illinois College of Medicine, and the Frederick G. and Elizabeth H. Nearing Scholar of the Grainger College of Engineering. His research interests include biomedical ultrasound, quantitative ultrasound imaging, ultrasound tomography, ultrasound-based therapy, beamforming, ultrasound-based devices and hardware, and communications using ultrasound. Dr. Oelze is currently a fellow of American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine (AIUM), the Acoustical Society of America (ASA), and the American Institute or Medical and Biological Engineering (AIMBE). He currently serves as an Associate Editor for IEEE Transactions on Ultrasonics, Ferroelectrics, and Frequency Control, Ultrasonic Imaging, and IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Engineering.</p></section><section id="bio8" class="bio"><p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/1272/13590064/76c939cd3cc6/fx8.webp" loading="lazy" id="lk0070" height="665" width="499" alt="graphic file with name fx8.webp"></p>
<p><strong>Rui Cao</strong> is currently an Assistant Professor with the Department of Biomedical Engineering, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH, USA, where he directs the SPOT Lab (Sound, Photoacoustic, Optical, and Translational imaging). He received the B.S. degree in optoelectronics and the M.S. degree in optical engineering from Nankai University, Tianjin, China, and the Ph.D. degree in biomedical engineering from the University of Virginia, Charlottesville, VA, USA. He was a Postdoctoral Scholar with the Andrew and Peggy Cherng Department of Medical Engineering at California Institute of Technology, Pasadena, CA, USA. His research interests include photoacoustic imaging, ultrafast ultrasound, wearable devices, and computational imaging with deep learning techniques.</p></section></section><section id="fn-group1" class="fn-group"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Footnotes</h2>
